 
 
 
 
 
IN THE SECOND JUDICIAL CIRCUIT OF FLORIDA 
OFFICE OF THE CHIEF JUDGE 
ADMINISTRATIVE ORDER 2026-02 
SECOND AMENDMENT 
 
IN RE:  CIRCUIT AND COUNTY JUDGE ASSIGNMENTS  
WHEREAS, Rule 2.215, Florida Rules of General Practice and Judicial Administration, 
provides that the Chief Judge shall assign judges to the courts and divisions and determine the length 
of each assignment along with designating administrative judges to assist in the supervision of the 
court; and 
WHEREAS, Administrative Order 2026-02, In Re: Circuit Judge and County Judge 
Assignments, as amended, currently sets forth the judicial assignments of the Second Judicial Circuit 
of Florida, it is therefore 
 
ORDERED, that the judicial assignments of the Second Judicial Circuit, as amended, are set 
out below: 
I. FRANKLIN COUNTY 
A. Circuit Judge Michael Beato shall be assigned all circuit cases and appeals from final 
administrative orders of local government code enforcement boards and of reviews and 
appeals as otherwise expressly provided by law. 
B. County Judge J. Gordon Shuler shall be assigned all county court cases, weekday first 
appearances, and shall serve as primary backup to the circuit judge. 
C. Magistrates assigned to Franklin County shall hear family cases with pro se parties, except 
for uncontested family cases, simplified dissolution of marriage cases, juvenile 
dependency, termination of parental rights trials, juvenile delinquency, and protective 
injunctions.   
II. GADSDEN COUNTY 
A. Circuit Judge Ronald W. Flury shall be assigned all circuit criminal cases. 
B. Circuit Judge Anthony Miller shall be assigned all circuit civil, family, probate, 
guardianship, dependency, and appeals from final administrative orders of local 
government code enforcement boards and of reviews and appeals as otherwise expressly 
provided by law. 
C. County Judge Kathy L. Garner shall be assigned all county court cases, weekday first 
appearances, and primary backup to assist with juvenile delinquency cases. 
D. Magistrates assigned to Gadsden County shall hear family cases with pro se parties, except 
for uncontested family cases, simplified dissolution of marriage cases, juvenile 
dependency, termination of parental rights trials, juvenile delinquency, and protective 
injunctions.   
 
 
 

